  nonce: 5b29  # 5b29
  message: "STALENESS AUDIT 2026-09-21 (owner-requested). VERDICT: NOT STALE, PREMISES HOLD, AND NOTHING HAS BEEN DONE. One premise is softened by work that landed elsewhere; it is named below so the row does not overstate.\n\nVERIFIED TODAY:\n  - The flag is live: `--include-non-production-sources` is declared at cli.py:10635 and read at cli.py:6418 / 6616 / 6638; cli.py:5852 documents that it widens BOTH filters.\n  - PYTHON IS A DEF/USE LANGUAGE. `registered_def_use_languages()` returns {go, javascript, python, rust, typescript}. This is the load-bearing premise -- it is what makes the 100%-structural result a puzzle rather than the known 0019 story -- and it is confirmed.\n    INSTRUMENT NOTE, because the first read was wrong and the wrong answer was plausible: calling `registered_def_use_languages()` on a bare import returns `frozenset()`. The registry is filled by import side effect (INV-zunik) and must be primed with `dataflow_scope.ensure_def_use_extractors_registered()` first. An empty set would have \"confirmed\" the 0019 explanation the row explicitly warns against assuming.\n  - NO FOLLOW-UP EXISTS. No tracker row cites WI-mojaz. Only measurement 0022 mentions the arm; 0023-0029 do not. This row still carries zero discussion entries and has not been updated since it was filed on 2026-09-09.\n  - THE FIRST THING TO DO IS STILL UNDONE: \"find out why python test code yields 100% structural findings when python is a def/use language.\"\n\nTHE ONE PREMISE THAT HAS SOFTENED. The row says a user gets a ~76%-wrong population \"with no indication in the output that the findings are unwalked -- except `walk_verdict`, which measurement 0006 already showed adjudicators do not see\". Since filing, docs/VERIFY-CLAIMS-SCOPE.md has published a section, \"`analysis_method` is not a confidence score\", that defines the three labels for a reader -- `ddg` the walk confirmed a data dependence, `ddg_mixed` the walk ran and did not confirm, `structural` NO REACHING-DEFINITION DATA WAS AVAILABLE -- and warns against filtering on the field, noting that on 0005 keeping only `ddg` would have discarded 7 of the 8 true positives.\n\nThat is disclosure in the published SCOPE, not in the output, so the row's complaint survives in its own terms: the finding itself still does not tell the reader it was unwalked. But the sentence should cite the doc rather than imply nothing anywhere says so. A reader who finds VERIFY-CLAIMS-SCOPE.md can now learn what `structural` means; they still cannot learn that this ARM is three-quarters structural.\n\nRELATED BUT NOT THE CAUSE, checked so it is not mistaken for one later: WI-tizig (now `wont_do`, published rather than fixed) establishes that a source read at MODULE TOP LEVEL can never be data-flow adjudicated and comes out `structural` for any language. That does not explain this arm -- the row's dominant shape is 152 of 224 at hops=0 with an ORM read and an ORM write INSIDE ONE TEST FUNCTION, which is function-anchored. The cause remains unestablished, exactly as filed.\n\nSTATUS STAYS `todo_hard`."  # 5b29
- op: discuss  # b790
  at: '2026-09-21T10:06:42Z'  # b790
  by: agent  # b790
  actor: jgstern_agent  # b790
  clock: 3  # b790
  nonce: b790  # b790
  message: "THE FIRST THING TO DO IS DONE (2026-09-21). The row said: \"find out why python test code yields 100% structural findings when python is a def/use language. Do not assume it is the same cause as 0019.\" It is NOT the 0019 cause. Python's extractor is registered AND IT RUNS. ITS OUTPUT IS DISCARDED AT THE LOOKUP BY A KEY THAT DOES NOT MATCH.\n\nTHE CHAIN, READ IN THE CODE RATHER THAN INFERRED.\n  taint.py:4740  walk_verdict_for(reached, ran=..., covered=fn_has_ddg)\n  taint.py:378   `unavailable` is returned when NOT ran and NOT covered\n  taint.py:4468  fn_has_ddg = source_fn in analyzed\n  taint.py:4232  analyzed = ddg_symbols\nSo `walk_verdict == \"unavailable\"` means exactly one thing: THE SOURCE FUNCTION'S ID IS NOT A KEY IN ddg_symbols. It does not mean the language lacks an extractor.\n\nMEASURED ON THIS REPOSITORY, both sides built from the same root:\n\n  analyzer callables                     36,360   (11,445 function + 24,915 method)\n  ddg_symbols                            29,525   kind slot ':function' on ALL of them\n  analyzer FUNCTIONS matched in ddg       7,912 of 11,445   69.1%\n  analyzer METHODS matched in ddg             0 of 24,915    0.0%\n\nZERO. Not low -- zero. 24,915 of 36,360 python callables (68.5%) can never have fn_has_ddg true, so every finding rooted in one is `unavailable` -> `structural` by construction, whatever the code does.\n\nAND THE WORK WAS ALREADY DONE. 21,257 of those 24,915 methods (85.3%) ARE in ddg_symbols -- under a key nothing asks for:\n\n  taint asks for : python:scripts/measure-factory-receiver-shape.py:138-153:_Scanner._qualified:method\n  ddg stored it  : python:scripts/measure-factory-receiver-shape.py:138-153:_qualified:function\n\nTwo slots differ: the NAME slot (bare vs class-qualified) and the KIND slot (function vs method). The reaching-definitions solve ran on those bodies and produced edges; the result is addressed under a name the consumer never constructs.\n\nTHIS IS DISCLOSED, AND THE DISCLOSURE UNDERSTATES IT. ddg_build.py's module docstring: \"The match is approximate in one disclosed way, inherited from the original: class context is not walked for Python, so a method is keyed as ...:function rather than ...:method.\" APPROXIMATE is the wrong word for a 0-of-24,915 match. Go is named in the same paragraph as the language that does it right.\n\nWHY DJANGO TEST CODE IS 100% STRUCTURAL, which is the row's actual question: a Django/unittest test is a METHOD on a TestCase subclass. Every one of them is in the 0% bucket. The arm the flag opens is test code, so the arm is 100% methods, so it is 100% structural -- and the structural fallback pairs any source with any sink inside a function, which is precisely the 152-of-224 hops=0 shape the row describes (an ORM create earlier, an assert on a get later, no dependence claimed between them).\n\nA SECOND, SMALLER MECHANISM, on the function side. ddg_build.py:279 inserts into ddg_symbols only `if result.ddg_edges:`. A function the solver analyzed and found NO data dependence in is absent, and absence is read as \"no reaching-definition data was available\". That is this project's absent-versus-empty defect at the DDG layer, and it accounts for part of the 31% of plain functions that also miss.\n\nTHE FIX HAS A PRECEDENT AND IS SMALL IN CODE. LanguageDdgSpec already carries `name_for` and `kind_for` hooks, and its own docstring says \"Go uses this to prepend a receiver type so method ids match the analyzer's.\" Python's spec (ddg_build.py:445) supplies NEITHER. This is a cure that shipped wired to one language.\n\nBUT THE FIX IS NOT OBVIOUSLY A PRECISION WIN, AND I AM NOT SHIPPING IT AS ONE. Restoring the lookup does not delete findings; it RELABELS them from `structural` to `ddg`/`ddg_mixed`. And docs/VERIFY-CLAIMS-SCOPE.md records, measured three times on three populations, that `ddg_mixed` scores BELOW `structural` for precision. So making 21,257 methods walkable could move this arm's 24.2% in either direction. It is an activation widening on a metric under INV-duvup's pre-registered regime, so it needs an A/B with every moved situation read at source -- the 0013-0019 pattern -- not a drive-by.\n\nFILED SEPARATELY as the buildable half. THIS ROW STAYS OPEN AND STAYS todo_hard: its subject is the arm's ~76% false-positive rate, and nothing here has changed that number. What has changed is that the cause is no longer unknown."  # b790
